Editorial metadata added by the Skribisto project
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

To demonstrate Skribisto's features, the project adds descriptive metadata to this
example project: scene synopses, tags, labels, character and story-bible notes,
aliases and point-of-view assignments.

The project also declares a workflow-status vocabulary, so that feature can be seen
working. No chapter is assigned a status: how finished a piece of Ken McConnell's
published novel is would be a judgement, not a description, and this metadata carries
no authority as to the author's intent.

All of it is declared in `starforgers.toml` beside this file, and is applied by

    cargo run -p skribisto-skrib-format --example build_example -- enrich \
        resources/examples/starforgers/starforgers.toml \
        resources/examples/starforgers/Starforgers.skrib

which asserts, every run, that the prose it read back is byte-identical to what it
writes out. A future edit to that tool therefore cannot quietly cross the line above.

This metadata is written by the Skribisto project, not by Ken McConnell. It is
editorial description of the novel, not part of it, and any error or infelicity in it
is the project's and not the author's. It carries no authority as to the author's
intent.

The prose files themselves (binders/*/text/*.scene.djot) are the author's work and
are untouched.
